1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:03,156
You lost a game, and you want to know why.

2
00:00:03,456 --> 00:00:08,611
AskTheMove reviews it move by move, with engine evidence you can check.

3
00:00:09,961 --> 00:00:14,806
Paste in the game. Here, Maya played Black and was mated in seven moves.

4
00:00:15,106 --> 00:00:19,940
One click imports it. Flip the board to your side and play it through to the mate.

5
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:25,030
Then Stockfish 18 reviews every move, for both players.

6
00:00:25,330 --> 00:00:30,513
It names the opening, the Philidor Defense, and graphs who was ahead.

7
00:00:30,813 --> 00:00:34,522
Every move gets a grade, and the key moves are pulled out for you.

8
00:00:35,873 --> 00:00:40,824
On move 5, Maya's bishop took White's queen. AskTheMove marks it a blunder.

9
00:00:41,124 --> 00:00:45,944
Why? After that capture, Stockfish finds mate in 2 for White.

10
00:00:46,244 --> 00:00:49,638
Bishop takes f7, check. King e7. Knight d5,

11
00:00:49,638 --> 00:00:53,253
mate. The line is right there on the card.

12
00:00:53,553 --> 00:00:58,096
The engine, the search time and the depth are printed, so you can check it.

13
00:00:59,446 --> 00:01:05,238
So what should Maya have played? Compare before the move: Stockfish wanted dxe5.

14
00:01:05,538 --> 00:01:10,272
Try that line yourself: 5…dxe5, then 6.Qxg4.

15
00:01:10,572 --> 00:01:15,826
Stockfish checks your line too: White is better, +1.89, but there is no mate.

16
00:01:17,176 --> 00:01:20,990
Then practice. Your key moves come back as positions to solve,

17
00:01:20,990 --> 00:01:22,704
with the answer hidden.

18
00:01:23,004 --> 00:01:25,694
Maya finds the engine's move in the first two.

19
00:01:25,994 --> 00:01:30,600
In the third, she grabs the queen again, and the review shows her why that fails.

20
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:34,836
One hint later, she plays dxe5. Found it.

21
00:01:36,186 --> 00:01:41,600
When you're ready, play a bot. There are 43, each with a rating and a personality.

22
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:45,545
Here it's Barnaby, rated 1050, on a 10-minute clock.

23
00:01:46,895 --> 00:01:50,882
There are guided lessons, 3,810 named openings,

24
00:01:50,882 --> 00:01:55,339
24 endgame positions and board vision drills too.

25
00:01:56,689 --> 00:02:01,925
Invited beta players can also ask an AI coach to explain any move,

26
00:02:01,925 --> 00:02:04,051
from the same evidence.

27
00:02:04,351 --> 00:02:09,698
AskTheMove. Join the beta at askthemove.online

